<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Rome</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1957">1957</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Treaty establishing the European Economic Community</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, signed on 25 March 1957 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Luxembourg, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>. It created a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a> and common market, with a commission, council, assembly and court, and took effect on 1 January 1958; a companion treaty set up Euratom. Much amended, it remains one of the founding treaties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>.</p></div>
